    {
      "id": "GENUS_ANTHROPOIDES",
      "name": "Anthropoides",
      "rank": "GENUS",
      "description": "Demoiselle cranes and blue cranes — elegant, relatively small cranes of dry steppes and semi-arid grasslands far removed from the wetland habitats favoured by most of their relatives. The demoiselle crane is the world's smallest crane, breeding across the central Asian steppes from the Black Sea to Mongolia and wintering in India. Its name comes from French — given by Marie Antoinette who was charmed by its dainty, graceful appearance — and it is indeed a bird of extraordinary elegance: grey-bodied with a long white ear-tuft, a black neck and breast, and long decorative tertial feathers that droop over the tail. Demoiselle cranes are rare but annual vagrants to Scandinavia, typically appearing in spring among flocks of common cranes at staging sites such as Lake Hornborga.",
      "lineage": "Steppe Cranes",
      "children": [
